```php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\User;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Validator;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Response;

class UserController extends Controller
{
    public function show($id)
    {
        $user = User::find($id);

        if (!$user) {
            return Response::json(['error' => 'User not found'], 404);
        }

        return Response::json($user);
    }
}
```

In this example, we've created a `UserController` with a `show` method that accepts an ID as a parameter. The method uses Laravel's `User` model to find the user in the database by their ID. If the user is not found, it returns a JSON response with an error message and a 404 status code. If the user is found, it returns a JSON response with the user data.

To use this function, you would need to have a route set up in your `routes/web.php` file that points to the `UserController@show` method, like so:

```php
Route::get('/user/{id}', 'UserController@show');
```

This route would allow you to access the user data by visiting `/user/{id}` in your web application. Replace `{id}` with the actual user ID you're interested in.

